course: dictionary
lesson: 6869
title: Too much excitement
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe feelings that have grown too big. Overexcited is one of them. The start of the word, over-, means too much, so overexcited means too excited. «"Overexcited" said about a child often means out of control.» Read the words around it to find the clue.
explain: When someone is overexcited, they may get too loud, too silly or too wild, and it becomes hard to stay calm. Parents and teachers often say it before birthdays, holidays or snow days. Ask yourself: has this excitement taken over and made someone lose control?
explain: Authors show overexcited feelings through actions. A character who jumps, shouts and cannot sit still is probably overexcited, even if the word is never used. To check your meaning, swap in "too excited to stay calm" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Overexcited is an adjective, a word that describes a person or thing.
fact: The prefix over- means too much, so overexcited means too excited.
fact: «Overexcited» said about a child often means out of control.
fact: Common phrases are "get overexcited" and "don't get overexcited".
fact: Being excited is a normal feeling; overexcited means the excitement has gone past the point of staying calm.
exercise: «Overexcited» said about a child often means what? | Very tired and sleepy | Quietly thoughtful | *Out of control | Hungry for dinner
exercise: On snow day, a character jumps, shouts and cannot sit still. Which word fits best? | Overdue | *Overexcited | Overflowing
exercise: What does the prefix over- mean in overexcited? | Again and again | Under, below | *Too much | Not at all
test: Which phrase might a teacher say to a class before a snow day? | Please write a poem about snow | Sit down and yawn | *Don't get overexcited, everyone | Please be sleepy and quiet
test: Which sentence uses overexcited correctly? | The soup was overexcited and too cold | The map was overexcited about the road | *The puppy was overexcited, running in wild circles when its owner came home
exam: What does the start of overexcited, over-, tell you? | Not at all | *Too much | Something in the past | Being done again
